  in more general ways there's not just  one mechanism to produce six protons but  there's various  and the leading ones are shown here we  have just looked at this  first one which is called gluon fusion  fusion channel so to clearance fuse  together to a higgs boson  there's this one here the second one  which is called vector boson fusion  bbf where we have the quarks  radiate two vector borons this is either  the z  border of the w boson and then those  coupled to the higgs field and produce  the higgs boson  we can also have associated production  where we have two quarks  we are again a zebo then or a w  both on radiating then with the both on  a w border  and generating a higgs and then  a very exciting one is the last one here  where the higgs boson  is produced in association with two top  quarks remember the top quark here has a  mass  of 175 gb the mass of the higgs boson is  125 gb  and so we have two of those so the scale  of this of this event is in the order of  500 ged  this plot here shows the production  cross section as function of center of  mass energy now the  large hydrogen collider the lhc operates  currently at 13 db  so the cross section we want to look at  are those so you see that the leading  cross section  which in the order of tenths of picobarn  is the one  where we have gluon fusion an order of  magnitude less is the one with vector  both  fusion and then we have associated  production  and then the last one is the one where  we have exposures produced in  association with top blocks  because the mass scale is much higher  the cross section for ttx is lower if  you increase the center  if you were to increase the center of  mass energy of the lhc you see this  rapid increase in cross sections just  because there is more phase space  available for this  for this production the coupling here  and the coupling here  is the same then the exposure and as i  said the case and we have already  discussed  expansion ratios or branching ratios in  general  and since the coupling is proportional  coupling of the  exposure into fermion is proportional to  the mass of the fermions you see  the dominant decay is the one into hex  to bb bar  and then you can find lower decays to  the taus  charm and to muons here you also have  the case into the vector bothans ww  and zz and so similar triangle  diagrams we have the case in the blue  glue and into two photons as well  so we have already measured the hex for  at 125 gev  and so those are the branching ratios as  predicted in the standard model

